# Instructions
Your task is to determine whether the compromised claim can be meaningfully rectified and, if so, produce a rectified version.

## 1. Assess Rectification Feasibility (1 Paragraph)
Assess critically whether a meaningful corrected version of the claim can be produced so that _any reasonable person/organization would ever publicly share the rectified claim as a standalone statement on social media._
Additionally, the rectified version needs to:
* keep the original claim’s topic,
* be a non-trivial statement, unlike “XY did not happen,” “this story is false,” and
* disclose manipulation/AI generation for fabricated media.
If feasibility is low or uncertain, stop after this paragraph.

## 2. Rectify the Claim
If clearly feasible, correct the claim into one single, intact, fact-checkable, standalone assertion that resolves the Compromised Reason. State the corrected fact directly; do not phrase it as a denial or negation and do not refer to (elements of) the original claim. Enclose the rectified claim in backticks, like `this`. Avoid any other Markdown. Formulate it not in the tone of everyday language.

## 3. Check the Rectification
For each rule below, critically determine if the rectification meets the stated requirements.

## 4. Regeneration of Rectification
If any requirement is violated, regenerate the rectification by providing a corrected version that meets all requirements. Again, enclose the rectified claim in backticks.

# Rules
a) Formulate the rectified claim as _one single, naked, and self-contained_ assertion that someone could fact-check. Avoid referring to "reports," "social media posts," "claims," "evidence," etc. Do not include any justifications.
b) Avoid composing multiple assertions. Output _only one single assertion_ in the rectification (even if there are multiple assertions to be corrected).
c) Make sure the rectified claim is clear by providing the necessary context. The claim _must stand on its own_ so that an average reader can understand it without further reading.
d) Keep the rectification concise and under {{ max_words }} words. Ideally, keep it even shorter.
e) Remove any details that are not necessary to understand.
f) Avoid negations and formulations implying falsehood ("not," "is false," etc.). Don't say what is wrong. Just state the truth itself.
g) Do NOT refer back to (elements of) the compromised version, e.g., do NOT write “[correct thing] rather than [wrong thing].”
h) If the Reason reveals fabrication of media (manipulation, deepfake...), state this explicitly.
i) If media were incorrectly contextualized, do NOT state that. Instead, state the correct context itself.
j) Keep the original language ({{ claim.language_name }}) of the claim.
k) Omit time specifications in the rectification, except for specifications long before, i.e., months or years before {{ claim.date_str }}.
l) If visuals are contained, refer to them in the claim.

